==Spanish==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|VL.|es}} *''potēre'', from {{etyl|la|es}} {{m|la|posse}}, present active infinitive of {{m|la|possum}}. Cognate with {{etyl|fr|-}} {{m|fr|pouvoir}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|[po̞ˈðe̞ɾ]|lang=es}}

===Noun===
{{es-noun|m|es}}

# [[power]], [[reign]]
# [[authorization]]
# [[proxy]] {{gloss|written certificate of authority to act for another}}

===Verb===
{{es-verb|pod|er|pres=puedo|pret=pude}}

# to [[be able]], [[can]]
#: {{ux|es|¿'''Puedes''' acompañarme mañana?|'''Could you''' come with me tomorrow?}}
#: {{ux|es|No '''puedes''' salir ya que no tienes permiso.|'''You can''''t go out since you're not allowed.}}
#: {{ux|es|No '''puedo''' hablar francés.|'''I can''''t speak French.}}
# {{label|es|El Salvador|colloquial|reflexive}} to be [[familiar]] with something, or with somebody's personality
#: {{ux|es|¿'''Te podés''' el número de tu prima?|'''Do you know''' your cousin's phone number?}}
#: {{ux|es|Como entrenador, tengo que '''poderme''' bien a todos mis jugadores.|As a coach, I must '''know''' my players really well.}}

====Conjugation====
{{es-conj-er (poder)}}

====Usage notes====
This verb is replaced with ''[[saber]]'' or ''[[ser capaz de]]'' for use with skills (as in to know how to do something) and omitted when relating to verbs of sense.
* "You are able to do it." – ''Puedes hacerlo'' (uses ''poder'')
* "She is able to swim." – ''Ella es capaz de nadar'' (uses ''ser capaz de'')
* "I am able to see it." – ''Lo veo'' (omitted)

